tical directions. Because the interaction kernel is even in each coordinate, the networks are parameterized in terms of the absolute displacements τ 1 = | x − ξ | 	au_{1}=|x-\xi| and τ 2 = | y − η | 	au_{2}=|y-\eta| . For MC-KAN, the shape constraints are enforced independently along each lag direction. For both models, the inputs are linearly normalized. During training, the differentiable solver uses the same spatial grid, time step, and numerical discretization as the reference solver. At each optimization step, the full solution field is computed on the 256 × 256 256	imes 256 grid and evaluated against the observations in 𝒟 \mathcal{D} . The loss is the MSE over the spatial observation points at each time snapshot, averaged over the snapshots. For evaluation, the learned spatial kernel is sampled on the same 256 × 256 256	imes 256 lag grid as the reference kernel, and the kernel reconstruction error is computed pointwise on this common grid. Complete numerical, architectural, and optimization settings are summarized in Table 10 . Table 5 reports the solution and kernel reconstruction errors across noise levels for the linear normalization. In the noise-free case, Cheb-KAN is marginally lower ( 2.64 % 2.64\% versus 3.64 % 3.64\% ). However, at every noisy level ( σ ≥ 0.02 \sigma\geq 0.02 ), MC-KAN achieves a lower kernel error ℰ ⁡ ( K ) \mathcal{E}(K) than Cheb-KAN, and the gap widens with noise, reaching nearly a factor of two at σ = 0.15 \sigma=0.15 ( 12.36 % 12.36\% versus 21.95 % 21.95\% ). The solution error ℰ ⁡ ( u ) \mathcal{E}(u) remains small for both methods and increases smoothly with noise. The two methods are comparable at low noise, with MC-KAN increasingly favored as noise grows. The solution error is an order of magnitude smaller than the kernel error, because the forward solution is less sensitive to small variations in the kernel than the inverse problem itself. The corresponding final-time solution reconstruction errors are shown in Appendix (F